<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Ferry network security configuration.

    WHY THIS EXISTS: at targetSdk 28+ Android blocks cleartext HTTP by default. Ferry had no
    declaration at all, so it inherited that block — and two features quietly depended on cleartext:

      * DACP reverse control (DacpClient) — an HTTP GET to the *sender's* own LAN address, which is
        how the TV remote pauses or skips what your iPhone is playing. Its failure path is
        runCatching { }.onFailure { Logger.e(...) }, so a blocked request logs and nothing else.
      * AirPlay video URL mode (AirPlayVideoPlayer) — the sender hands over a media URL for the TV
        to fetch, and those URLs are routinely http:// on the local network.

    WHY PERMITTING IT COSTS NOTHING REAL HERE: this setting governs the platform HTTP stack
    (HttpURLConnection, MediaPlayer, WebView). It does not govern raw sockets, which is what every
    AirPlay protocol port already uses — RTSP on 7000, mirror video on 7100, audio on 6001. Those
    are cleartext by protocol design and were never affected by this flag. So the block was not
    protecting the bulk of Ferry's traffic; it was only breaking the two features above.

    There is also nothing else to protect: Ferry has no hardcoded remote endpoint anywhere (see
    AUDIT.md), needs no internet connection, and the only host it ever contacts is the sender that
    connected to it, resolved via mDNS on the local network.

    Android's config format matches on hostnames, not address ranges, so "RFC1918 only" cannot be
    expressed here — the sender's address is whatever DHCP handed it. Hence a base-config.
-->
<!--
    InsecureBaseConfiguration is suppressed here and NOT in the project's lint disable list, so the
    check stays live for anything else that might turn cleartext on later. This one instance is the
    considered exception documented above.
-->
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <base-config
        cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"
        tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
